<h2 id="morphisms-section-quasi-projective" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="01VV">29.41</span> Quasi-projective morphisms</h2>

<p>
The discussion in the previous section suggests the following definitions. We take our definition of quasi-projective from <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/EGA">EGA</a>]</span>. The version with the letter “H” is the definition in <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/H">H</a>]</span>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="01VW">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01VW">Definition <span data-tag="01VW">29.41.1</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say $f$ is <em>quasi-projective</em> if $f$ is of finite type and there exists an $f$-relatively ample invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $f$ is <em>H-quasi-projective</em> if there exists a quasi-compact immersion $X \to \mathbf{P}^ n_ S$ over $S$ for some $n$.<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000651566" id="a0000651566-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $f$ is <em>locally quasi-projective</em> if there exists an open covering $S = \bigcup V_ j$ such that each $f^{-1}(V_ j) \to V_ j$ is quasi-projective. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
As this definition suggests the property of being quasi-projective is not local on $S$. At a later stage we will be able to say more about the category of quasi-projective schemes, see More on Morphisms, Section <a href="/tag/0B41" data-tag="0B41">37.49</a>. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0B3G">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B3G">Lemma <span data-tag="0B3G">29.41.2</span>.</a> A base change of a quasi-projective morphism is quasi-projective. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from Lemmas <a href="/tag/01T4" data-tag="01T4">29.15.4</a> and <a href="/tag/0893" data-tag="0893">29.38.9</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0C4M">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C4M">Lemma <span data-tag="0C4M">29.41.3</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to S$ be morphisms of schemes. If $S$ is quasi-compact and $f$ and $g$ are quasi-projective, then $g \circ f$ is quasi-projective. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from Lemmas <a href="/tag/01T3" data-tag="01T3">29.15.3</a> and <a href="/tag/0C4K" data-tag="0C4K">29.38.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="01VX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01VX">Lemma <span data-tag="01VX">29.41.4</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. If $f$ is quasi-projective, or H-quasi-projective or locally quasi-projective, then $f$ is separated of finite type. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="01VY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01VY">Lemma <span data-tag="01VY">29.41.5</span>.</a> A H-quasi-projective morphism is quasi-projective. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="01VZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01VZ">Lemma <span data-tag="01VZ">29.41.6</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
The morphism $f$ is locally quasi-projective. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
There exists an open covering $S = \bigcup V_ j$ such that each $f^{-1}(V_ j) \to V_ j$ is H-quasi-projective. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/01VY" data-tag="01VY">29.41.5</a> we see that (2) implies (1). Assume (1). The question is local on $S$ and hence we may assume $S$ is affine, $X$ of finite type over $S$ and $\mathcal{L}$ is a relatively ample invertible sheaf on $X/S$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/01VT" data-tag="01VT">29.40.4</a> we may assume $\mathcal{L}$ is ample on $X$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/01VS" data-tag="01VS">29.40.3</a> we see that there exists an immersion of $X$ into a projective space over $S$, i.e., $X$ is H-quasi-projective over $S$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0B3H">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B3H">Lemma <span data-tag="0B3H">29.41.7</span>.</a> A quasi-affine morphism of finite type is quasi-projective. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0891" data-tag="0891">29.38.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="0C4N">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C4N">Lemma <span data-tag="0C4N">29.41.8</span>.</a> Let $g : Y \to S$ and $f : X \to Y$ be morphisms of schemes. If $g \circ f$ is quasi-projective and $f$ is quasi-compact<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000651690" id="a0000651690-mark"><sup>2</sup></a>, then $f$ is quasi-projective. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Observe that $f$ is of finite type by Lemma <a href="/tag/01T8" data-tag="01T8">29.15.8</a>. Thus the lemma follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0C4L" data-tag="0C4L">29.38.10</a> and the definitions. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
